---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { dogSizeBands, estimateDogHumanAge, getDogSizeLabel, getDogLifeStage, getDogSeniorAge, lifespanRanges } from '../../../utils/agingModel';
const title = 'Dog Years to Human Years: How Age Estimates Work';
const description = 'Understand size-aware dog-to-human age estimates, why ×7 is too simple, what a 10-year-old dog’s estimate means, and how life stages differ from human years.';
const faqs = [
  { q: 'How old is a 10-year-old dog in human years?', a: `In this site’s educational model: ${dogSizeBands.map(b => `${b.en}: about ${estimateDogHumanAge(10, b.size)}`).join('; ')} human-equivalent years. These rounded comparisons are not a medical age or a remaining-lifespan prediction.` },
  { q: 'Why not multiply dog age by seven?', a: 'A fixed multiplier cannot represent faster early development and size differences in later years. Our model uses separate first-year, second-year and later-year comparisons.' },
  { q: 'Does a high human-age estimate mean my dog is senior?', a: 'Not by itself. Human-equivalent age is a separate output. Our senior-stage reference uses the midpoint of a size-based lifespan assumption and the final approximately 25% concept described by AAHA.' },
  { q: 'Can this estimate tell me how healthy my dog is?', a: 'No. Dogs of the same age and size can have different health and needs. Discuss symptoms or changes with a veterinarian rather than relying on a human-age number.' },
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} lang="en" breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Blog', url: '/en/blog/' }, { label: 'Dog Years Explained', url: '/en/blog/dog-human-years/' }]}>
  <article class="w-[calc(100vw-4rem)] max-w-3xl mx-auto sm:px-6 py-10 text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
    <header class="mb-8">
      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 mb-4">{title}</h1>
      <p class="text-lg">Dog-to-human age numbers are comparisons, not measurements of biological age. A 10-year-old dog does not have one universal human age: this site’s estimate varies by adult size. Here is how to interpret the model and its limits.</p>
      <p class="mt-4">Want a result for your dog now? <a href="/en/tools/dog-age-calculator/" class="text-blue-700 underline">Enter age and adult size in the Dog Age Calculator</a>. This article explains the result rather than providing a second calculator.</p>
    </header>
    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">Why the “multiply by 7” shortcut is too simple</h2>
      <p>A single multiplier treats every year and every size alike. Pet Aging Hub instead uses a segmented, size-aware educational comparison. This is a consistent site convention, not a scientifically exact conversion or a veterinary formula.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">How the site’s estimate is calculated</h2>
      <p>Up to age 1, the estimate scales toward the first-year value. Between ages 1 and 2, it interpolates between the first- and second-year values. After age 2, it adds the later-year increment for each additional calendar year. Decimal inputs use the same segments; only the final human-equivalent result is rounded.</p>
      <div class="max-w-full overflow-x-auto mt-4 border border-slate-200 rounded-xl" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Model values by dog size">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[570px] text-sm text-left">
          <caption class="p-3 text-left">Model values generated from the shared calculator functions</caption>
          <thead class="bg-slate-100"><tr><th scope="col" class="p-3">Adult size</th><th scope="col" class="p-3">At 1 year</th><th scope="col" class="p-3">At 2 years</th><th scope="col" class="p-3">Added per later year</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{dogSizeBands.map(({ size }) => <tr class="border-t"><th scope="row" class="p-3">{getDogSizeLabel(size, 'en')}</th><td class="p-3">{estimateDogHumanAge(1, size)}</td><td class="p-3">{estimateDogHumanAge(2, size)}</td><td class="p-3">{estimateDogHumanAge(3, size) - estimateDogHumanAge(2, size)}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-4">For age a after 2 years: second-year value + (a − 2) × later-year increment. Earlier ages are interpolated as described above. These coefficients are Pet Aging Hub approximations; institutions cited for life stages do not supply or endorse them.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">How old is a 10-year-old dog in human years?</h2>
      <p>{dogSizeBands.map(b => `${b.en}: about ${estimateDogHumanAge(10, b.size)} human-equivalent years`).join('; ')}. These are different educational comparisons for the same calendar age, not different diagnoses.</p>
      <div class="max-w-full overflow-x-auto mt-4 border border-slate-200 rounded-xl" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Dog age reference chart">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[540px] text-sm text-left"><caption class="p-3 text-left">Selected ages in the same model</caption>
          <thead class="bg-slate-100"><tr><th scope="col" class="p-3">Dog age</th>{dogSizeBands.map(b => <th scope="col" class="p-3">{b.en}</th>)}</tr></thead>
          <tbody>{[1, 2, 5, 10, 15].map(age => <tr class="border-t"><th scope="row" class="p-3">{age}</th>{dogSizeBands.map(b => <td class="p-3">{estimateDogHumanAge(age, b.size)}</td>)}</tr>)}</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">Why adult size matters—and what breed adds</h2>
      <p>Size is useful context, not a complete explanation of aging. Breed, genetics, health and care introduce individual differences. Use expected adult weight for a puppy or mixed-breed dog rather than selecting from an assumed breed stereotype. See <a class="text-blue-700 underline" href="/en/blog/dog-aging-by-breed/">how dog aging varies by size and breed</a>.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">Human-equivalent age is not the senior-stage definition</h2>
      <p>AAHA describes dog senior status in terms of the final approximately 25% of estimated lifespan. The site uses 75% of the midpoint of its general size-based lifespan assumptions as a separate stage reference; it does not classify senior status from the human-age output.</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-5 mt-3">{dogSizeBands.map(({ size, en: label }) => <li>{label}: site lifespan assumption {lifespanRanges.dog[size].join('–')} years; senior reference around {Math.round(getDogSeniorAge(size))} years. At age 10, the model label is {getDogLifeStage(10, size)}.</li>)}</ul>
      <p class="mt-3">The rounded ages above are approximate descriptions, not exact clinical cutoffs. A dog’s needs can change before or after them. Learn about <a href="/en/blog/dog-aging-signs/" class="text-blue-700 underline">signs of aging in dogs</a> and <a href="/en/blog/senior-dog-care/" class="text-blue-700 underline">senior dog care principles</a>.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      {faqs.map(({ q, a }) => <details class="border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-4 mb-3"><summary class="font-semibold cursor-pointer">{q}</summary><p class="mt-3">{a}</p></details>)}
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">Source and model limitations</h2>
      <p><a href="https://www.aaha.org/resources/2023-aaha-senior-care-guidelines-for-dogs-and-cats/defining-the-senior-patient/" class="text-blue-700 underline">AAHA: Defining the Senior Patient</a> supports life-stage context only. The human-age equation, weight bands and numerical lifespan assumptions are this site’s educational model, not an AAHA or AVMA formula.</p>
      <p class="mt-3">This article is educational and does not provide veterinary advice. Age estimates cannot diagnose illness or predict how long a particular dog will live. For the wider context, read the <a href="/en/blog/pet-aging-guide/" class="text-blue-700 underline">pet aging guide</a>.</p>
    </section>
    <footer class="mt-8 border-t border-slate-200 pt-4 text-sm text-slate-600">Published June 25, 2026. Model explanation updated September 30, 2026.</footer>
  </article>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({ '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'FAQPage', mainEntity: faqs.map(({ q, a }) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: a } })) })} />
</BaseLayout>
